RUNG 1 -- Fn: the body only READS the capture
  call_fn(reads)     hello / hello
  Callable many times, and callable through a shared reference.
  `greeting` is still ours: hello

RUNG 2 -- FnMut: the body MUTATES the capture
  call_fn_mut(counts) (1, 2)
  Callable many times, but the caller needs `&mut` to do it --
  which is why call_fn_mut takes `mut f`. A FnMut is NOT an Fn:
  handing this to call_fn would not compile.

RUNG 3 -- FnOnce: the body CONSUMES the capture
  call_fn_once(consumes) consumed
  It gives the String away, so there is nothing left to give a
  second time. Calling it twice is a move-out-of-a-moved-value
  error, and the trait bound is the compiler recording that.

THE LADDER, NOT THE MENU
  Fn      => also FnMut => also FnOnce
  So a function taking FnOnce accepts all three, and one taking
  Fn accepts only the first. Take the LOOSEST bound your body
  needs -- FnOnce if you call it once -- and you accept the most
  callers. That is the same reasoning as taking &str over String.

WHAT DOES NOT DECIDE THE RUNG
  `move` does not. It says where the captured values live, not
  what the body does with them: `move || println!("{}", n)` on a
  Copy type is still an Fn. The body is the whole test.
